{"id":"storage-ephemeral-volumes","corpus":"k8s","title":"Ephemeral Volumes","sourceUrl":"https://kubernetes.io/docs/concepts/storage/ephemeral-volumes/","license":"Kubernetes Documentation © The Kubernetes Authors, licensed CC BY 4.0","licenseUrl":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/","blocks":[{"kind":"h1","text":"Ephemeral Volumes"},{"kind":"p","text":"This document describes ephemeral volumes in Kubernetes. Familiarity with volumes is suggested, in particular PersistentVolumeClaim and PersistentVolume."},{"kind":"p","text":"Some applications need additional storage but don't care whether that data is stored persistently across restarts. For example, caching services are often limited by memory size and can move infrequently used data into storage that is slower than memory with little impact on overall performance."},{"kind":"p","text":"Other applications expect some read-only input data to be present in files, like configuration data or secret keys."},{"kind":"p","text":"Ephemeral volumes are designed for these use cases. Because volumes follow the Pod's lifetime and get created and deleted along with the Pod, Pods can be stopped and restarted without being limited to where some persistent volume is available."},{"kind":"p","text":"Ephemeral volumes are specified inline in the Pod spec, which simplifies application deployment and management."},{"kind":"h3","text":"Types of ephemeral volumes"},{"kind":"p","text":"Kubernetes supports several different kinds of ephemeral volumes for different purposes:"},{"kind":"li","text":"emptyDir: empty at Pod startup, with storage coming locally from the kubelet base directory (usually the root disk) or RAM"},{"kind":"li","text":"configMap, downwardAPI, secret: inject different kinds of Kubernetes data into a Pod"},{"kind":"li","text":"image: allows mounting container image files or artifacts, directly to a Pod."},{"kind":"li","text":"CSI ephemeral volumes: similar to the previous volume kinds, but provided by special CSI drivers which specifically support this feature"},{"kind":"li","text":"generic ephemeral volumes, which can be provided by all storage drivers that also support persistent volumes"},{"kind":"p","text":"emptyDir, configMap, downwardAPI, secret are provided as local ephemeral storage. They are managed by kubelet on each node."},{"kind":"p","text":"CSI ephemeral volumes must be provided by third-party CSI storage drivers."},{"kind":"p","text":"Generic ephemeral volumes can be provided by third-party CSI storage drivers, but also by any other storage driver that supports dynamic provisioning. Some CSI drivers are written specifically for CSI ephemeral volumes and do not support dynamic provisioning: those then cannot be used for generic ephemeral volumes."},{"kind":"p","text":"The advantage of using third-party drivers is that they can offer functionality that Kubernetes itself does not support, for example storage with different performance characteristics than the disk that is managed by kubelet, or injecting different data."},{"kind":"h3","text":"CSI ephemeral volumes"},{"kind":"p","text":"CSI ephemeral volumes are only supported by a subset of CSI drivers. The Kubernetes CSI Drivers list shows which drivers support ephemeral volumes."},{"kind":"p","text":"Conceptually, CSI ephemeral volumes are similar to configMap, downwardAPI and secret volume types: the storage is managed locally on each node and is created together with other local resources after a Pod has been scheduled onto a node. Kubernetes has no concept of rescheduling Pods anymore at this stage. Volume creation has to be unlikely to fail, otherwise Pod startup gets stuck. In particular, storage capacity aware Pod scheduling is not supported for these volumes. They are currently also not covered by the storage resource usage limits of a Pod, because that is something that kubelet can only enforce for storage that it manages itself."},{"kind":"p","text":"Here's an example manifest for a Pod that uses CSI ephemeral storage:"},{"kind":"p","text":"The volumeAttributes determine what volume is prepared by the driver. These attributes are specific to each driver and not standardized. See the documentation of each CSI driver for further instructions."},{"kind":"h3","text":"CSI driver restrictions"},{"kind":"p","text":"CSI ephemeral volumes allow users to provide volumeAttributes directly to the CSI driver as part of the Pod spec. A CSI driver allowing volumeAttributes that are typically restricted to administrators is NOT suitable for use in an inline ephemeral volume. For example, parameters that are normally defined in the StorageClass should not be exposed to users through the use of inline ephemeral volumes."},{"kind":"p","text":"Cluster administrators who need to restrict the CSI drivers that are allowed to be used as inline volumes within a Pod spec may do so by:"},{"kind":"li","text":"Removing Ephemeral from volumeLifecycleModes in the CSIDriver spec, which prevents the driver from being used as an inline ephemeral volume."},{"kind":"li","text":"Using an admission webhook to restrict how this driver is used."},{"kind":"h3","text":"Generic ephemeral volumes"},{"kind":"p","text":"Generic ephemeral volumes are similar to emptyDir volumes in the sense that they provide a per-pod directory for scratch data that is usually empty after provisioning. But they may also have additional features:"},{"kind":"li","text":"Storage can be local or network-attached."},{"kind":"li","text":"Volumes can have a fixed size that Pods are not able to exceed."},{"kind":"li","text":"Volumes may have some initial data, depending on the driver and parameters."}],"lemmas":{"ephemerals":["ephemeral","ephemerals"],"volumes":["volume","volumes"],"documents":["document","documents"],"documenting":["documenting","document"],"documented":["documented","document"],"describes":["describes","describe"],"described":["described","describe"],"describing":["describing","describe"],"kubernetes":["kuber